.TH RTC 3
.SH NAME
rtc \- real-time clock and non-volatile RAM
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind #r /dev
.PP
.B /dev/rtc
.br
.B /dev/nvram
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I rtc
device supports devices
with real-time clocks and non-volatile RAM.
.PP
The
.B rtc
file behaves just like
.BR /dev/time
(see
.IR cons (3)).
The real-time clock is maintained on-board;
.B /dev/time
is set from the file server.  Neither is necessarily more accurate.
.PP
The
.B nvram
file provides (if permission allows)
access to the local non-volatile RAM.
For example,
.IR boot (8)
reads the machine's key
from there
(see
.IR auth (8)).
.SS ET-SoC-1 Maxion
The Maxion has neither a calendar clock nor non-volatile RAM, so it has no
.BR nvram .
Its
.B rtc
is the free-running CLINT
.B mtime
counter (the device-tree timebase, about 9.33 MHz),
which only a cold reset of the card restarts,
plus an epoch.
The epoch comes from one sample taken by the PCIe host:
the counter value and the Unix time at which the host read it,
in the device tree as
.B /chosen
.B ainekko,rtc-sample
(four cells: counter high and low, nanoseconds high and low),
with its uncertainty in nanoseconds in
.BR ainekko,rtc-error-ns .
The sample remains valid across guest reboots until the next cold reset;
a sample ahead of the counter is ignored.
At boot the kernel sets the time of day from the clock,
to the nanosecond;
without a sample the clock counts from the epoch at the last cold reset.
Writing
.B rtc
moves only the epoch, and only until the next boot.
.PP
The
.B rtcctl
file reads as the clock's state:
the source of the epoch
.RB ( host ,
.B write
or
.BR "cold reset" ),
the epoch and its counter value,
the uncertainty, the timebase, the counter, the clock in nanoseconds,
and the time of day set at boot.
Writing
.B tod
to it sets the time of day from the clock to the nanosecond,
where copying
.B rtc
to
.B /dev/time
truncates it to the second.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR cons (3),
.IR mxp (3),
.IR auth (8),
.IR boot (8),
.IR timesync (8)
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/9/*/devrtc.c
